Q: Byte array to Hex string conversion in javascript A: <p>As of September 2025 <code>.toHex()</code> is supported among major browsers.<br />
You can use it like this (<a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Uint8Array/toHex" rel="nofollow noreferrer">source</a>):</p>
<pre><code>const uint8Array = new Uint8Array([202, 254, 208, 13]);
console.log(uint8Array.toHex()); // &quot;cafed00d&quot;

const data = new Uint8Array([255, 0, 0, 0, 255, 0, 0, 0, 255]);
for (let i = 0; i &lt; data.length; i += 3) {
  console.log(data.slice(i, i + 3).toHex());
}
// &quot;ff0000&quot;
// &quot;00ff00&quot;
// &quot;0000ff&quot;
</code></pre>
<p><em><sub>Code is directly copied from mdn</sub></em></p>

Q: Why use apparently meaningless do-while and if-else statements in macros? A: <p>@jfm3 - You have a nice answer to the question.  You might also want to add that the macro idiom also prevents the possibly more dangerous (because there's no error) unintended behavior with simple 'if' statements:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>#define FOO(x)  f(x); g(x)

if (test) FOO( baz);
</code></pre>
<p>expands to:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>if (test) f(baz); g(baz);
</code></pre>
<p>which is syntactically correct so there's no compiler error, but has the probably unintended consequence that g() will always be called.</p>
<p>Update 2026-02-12:</p>
<p>To address why the <code>do { ... } while (0)</code> syntax is usually preferred over simply using braces, it's because that lets the macro be used any place a regular function can be used.  This lets you use a macro to 'override' a function such as for debugging.</p>
<p>There is at least one situation where the &quot;braces only&quot; style of macro cannot be used where a normal function call could be - as the non-compound statement following an <code>if</code> condition:</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;

#if 1
  // use braces only
  #define FOOBAR(x)     { printf( &quot;%s &quot;, &quot;foo?&quot;); printf( &quot;%i\n&quot;, (x)); }
#else
  // use do { ... } while(0)
  #define FOOBAR(x)  do { printf( &quot;%s &quot;, &quot;foo!&quot;); printf( &quot;%i\n&quot;, (x)); } while (0)
#endif

int main()
{
    if (1)
        FOOBAR(42);
    else
        printf(&quot;just a dummy statement\n&quot;);

    return 0;
}
</code></pre>
<p>Compiling the above code results in a syntax error, Something like &quot;error: 'else' without a previous 'if'&quot;.</p>
<p>If you enable the second form of the macro, it compiles and runs fine.</p>

Q: Difference between DECIMAL and NUMERIC datatypes in PostgreSQL A: <p>As indicated in the source code for Postgres (GitHub mirror), <code>decimal</code> and <code>dec</code> are aliases for the underlying type <code>numeric</code>. The documentation for the <code>numeric</code> datatype claims, &quot;The types <code>decimal</code> and <code>numeric</code> are equivalent.&quot; Not only are these two types <em>functionally equivalent</em> (implementing the same methods and behaviors), but <strong>the two types are the same type</strong>. All references to the <code>decimal</code> type are references to the <code>numeric</code> data type. Furthermore, the type <code>dec</code> is also an alias of the type <code>numeric</code>.</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>| DECIMAL_P opt_type_modifiers
    {
        $$ = SystemTypeName(&quot;numeric&quot;);
        $$-&gt;typmods = $2;
        $$-&gt;location = @1;
    }
| DEC opt_type_modifiers
    {
        $$ = SystemTypeName(&quot;numeric&quot;);
        $$-&gt;typmods = $2;
        $$-&gt;location = @1;
    }
| NUMERIC opt_type_modifiers
    {
        $$ = SystemTypeName(&quot;numeric&quot;);
        $$-&gt;typmods = $2;
        $$-&gt;location = @1;
    }
</code></pre>
<p>The above code is an excerpt from lines 14808 to 14825 of <a href="https://github.com/postgres/postgres/blob/master/src/backend/parser/gram.y" rel="nofollow noreferrer">gram.y</a> in PostgreSQL 18.2. This code is available from The PostgreSQL Global Development Group and The Regents of the University of California under the <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/about/licence/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">PostgreSQL License</a>. A GitHub mirror of the full source code can be found <a href="https://github.com/postgres/postgres" rel="nofollow noreferrer">here</a>.</p>
